[COMMENTS]
STATION,Station opened 1973 as a velocity area station. In 1977 a concrete Flat-V weir and high-flow gauging bridge were constructed. Masonry hut on right bank housing stilling well with main and backup instruments both using shaft encoders with float and weight. Channel width at stations c.9m. GENERAL: Flat V weir station gauging a natural catchment with significant storage in headwater loughs. STATION HYDROMETRY: Flows contained. Weir requires cleaning in summer, though little deposition of material behind weir. Two peak flow ratings apply to period of record. High flow rating from current meter gaugings; simple extrapolation above 1.4m. More medium-flow gaugings required. Prior to weir installation rating based on current meter gaugings. FLOW RECORD: Daily flows from start of 1980 reprocessed in 2002. Water year 1971/1972 rejected because AMAX is from partial water year at start of station record. Full period of record peak flow data reviewed and released in August 2021 (WINFAP Files v10) and in August 2025 (WINFAP Files v14). FLOW REGIME: Natural flow regime; significant storage in headwater loughs.
CATCHMENT,Solid geology - Silurian greywacke sandstones and shales form indurated bedrock. Drift geology - upstream, rock near surface and drumlinised glacial till overlain by limited river alluvium (terraced). Downstream, extensive glacial sand and gravel complex. Predominantly improved grassland with some horticulture (mainly potatoes). No significant urban development.
Qmed Suitability,Maximum gauged flow 1.3 times QMED for Flat V weir, although for the velocity area station prior to weir installation ratio was not as favourable. Flows are contained at QMED.
Pooling Suitability,Extrapolation above highest gauging deemed satisfactory due to permanency of concrete section and all flows fully contained.
[END]
